<p>NASA says March 6 is the earliest chance to launch Artemis II, the mission that would send four astronauts on a trip around the Moon and back, after a successful “wet dress rehearsal” with its Space Launch System rocket. NASA officials stressed that March 6 is only a target. The agency still has work to finish and reviews to clear before it sets a firm launch day. If the mission is cleared to fly, Artemis II would mark a major step in human spaceflight—and another sign that NASA is building momentum toward longer-term operations around the Moon.</p><h3>Highlights:</h3><ul><li>Milestone test: Mercopress said NASA’s latest run was its second full countdown-and-fueling rehearsal of the SLS rocket—an important pre-launch exercise meant to validate procedures from start to finish.</li><li>Leak check: NASA Administrator Jared Isaacman said the teams made “major progress” from the first attempt, which was disrupted by hydrogen leaks. The second test finished without significant seepage.</li></ul><h3>Perspectives:</h3><ul><li>NASA: NASA said March 6 is only the earliest possible date. The agency will set a firm launch day only after it finishes the remaining pre-launch work and reviews. (<a href='https://www.straitstimes.com/world/united-states/nasa-says-march-6-earliest-date-for-artemis-2-lunar-launch'>The Straits Times</a>)</li><li>NASA Administrator Jared Isaacman: Isaacman pointed to the successful fueling test as a sign of progress and tied it to a broader push to return humans to the lunar environment. (<a href='https://www.scmp.com/news/world/united-states-canada/article/3344184/nasa-aims-send-astronauts-moon-march-after-successful-test'>South China Morning Post</a>)</li></ul><h3>Sources:</h3><ul><li><a href='https://en.mercopress.com/2026/02/21/nasa-targets-march-6-for-artemis-ii-after-passing-key-sls-fueling-rehearsal'>NASA targets March 6 for Artemis II after passing key SLS fueling rehearsal</a> - mercopress.com</li><li><a href='https://www.straitstimes.com/wo